    description: "Extend the L3 producer-coherence walker (packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/producer_coherence.py) to statically resolve dict-indirection / tuple-unpack edge_type emit sites. Currently find_emitted_edge_types / _resolve_function_local / _resolve_simple_rhs handle ast.Name targets + literal/ternary RHS but SKIP (a) ast.Tuple unpack targets (e.g. 'a, b = MAP[k]') and (b) Subscript RHS ('MAP[k]'). This was the blind-spot behind WI-rorul: the OTP linker shipped otp_call/otp_cast via 'handler_suffix, edge_type = CALL_TYPE_MAP[call_type]', unregistered yet invisible to the static gate (test_no_strict_producer_violations_for_edge_type stayed green). WI-rorul FOLDED the OTP values to dispatches_to + meta['mechanism'] (PR set post-merge), removing the only known exploiter (the scope-sweep's claimed SCIP has_type/defined_by could NOT be reproduced). So this is now PREVENTIVE hardening with ZERO current exploiter. Implement: resolve a tuple-unpack 'x, y = <dict-subscript>' by (1) resolving the dict literal (module/local constant via _resolve_module_constant), (2) taking the union of the i-th tuple element across all dict values for unpack position i; conservatively return None (silent skip, no false positives) if any value is not a resolvable literal tuple of strings. Add a fixture-based test: a synthetic emitter using 'et = SOME_MAP[k]' with an UNregistered edge_type value must now be FLAGGED by the gate. Keep the conservative posture (any unresolvable RHS poisons resolution → None). Per wave3 plan §A WI-rorul 'extend the L3 gate walker to resolve dict-indirection (the execution half)'. Split from WI-rorul to keep the fold (vocabulary half) and the static-analyzer hardening (execution half) as separate reviewable PRs."  # 16e5
    fields: {}  # 16e5
